## GET / and the not-found page.

import { Page, repository } from "~/app/ui/page"
import samples, { Sample } from "~/app/samples"
import highlight from "~/app/highlight"
import markdown, { Chapter } from "~/app/markdown"
import manual from "~/app/pages/manual"
import http, { Request, Response } from "std/http"

export fn page(request!: Request) -> Response {
    http.htmlResponse(
        <Page title="Thoxa" request={request} current="home">
            <main class="home">
                {intro()}
                {design()}
                {features()}
                {sampleSection(samples.load())}
                {status()}
                {building()}
            </main>
        </Page>
    )
}

export fn missing(request!: Request) -> Response {
    http.htmlResponseStatus(
        404,
        "Not Found",
        <Page title="Not found · Thoxa" request={request}>
            <main class="home">
                <h1>Not found</h1>
                <p>There is no page at this address. Try the <a href="/">home page</a> or the <a href="/manual">manual</a>.</p>
            </main>
        </Page>
    )
}

fn intro() -> Node {
    <header class="intro">
        <h1>Thoxa</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            A statically typed, expression-oriented programming language. The
            compiler translates Thoxa to C, and a C compiler builds that into a
            native executable.
        </p>
        <p class="facts">
            <span>Pre-alpha</span>
            <span>Linux x86-64</span>
            <span>No releases yet</span>
            <a href="/manual">Manual</a>
            <a href={repository}>Source</a>
        </p>
    </header>
}

fn design() -> Node {
    <section class="design" id="design">
        <h2>Design</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>Automatic memory, without a collector or a borrow checker.</strong>
            Values are reference counted. Integers, floats, and small records are stored
            inline; strings, arrays, and maps are counted and copied on write, so a value
            never changes under another binding. There are no lifetimes and no collection
            pauses. Files, sockets, and databases close when their last reference goes.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Errors are values.</strong>
            <code>{"Trial<T, E>"}</code> and <code>{"May<T>"}</code> are ordinary unions from the
            standard library, and <code>?</code> hands a failure back to the caller. There
            are no exceptions. A panic (an index out of range, a division by zero) is for
            bugs, and it ends only the task it happens in.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>The type is the schema.</strong>
            <code>{"Int64 where >= 1"}</code> is a type, and <code>{"\"small\" | \"large\""}</code> is a
            closed set of strings. From a type definition the compiler writes a JSON reader
            that reports every bad field, a form reader, and an encoder. There are no derive
            macros, schema objects, or runtime reflection.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Close to C.</strong>
            The output is C. <code>extern fn</code> calls a C function directly, a type with
            body fields has C layout, and integer behaviour is defined: arithmetic wraps,
            narrowing is checked, and division by zero panics instead of being undefined.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>What a small web service needs is in the language.</strong>
            Tag literals build HTML as values, a type body of <code>{"GET \"/path\""}</code> clauses
            is a router, <code>{"sql\"…\""}</code> quotes every value it interpolates, and
            <code>{"-->"}</code> starts a task whose socket operations park instead of blocking:
            one OS thread, no <code>async</code>, no <code>await</code>. The standard library has
            an HTTP/1.1 server and SQLite. This site is a Thoxa program, and its manual page is
            rendered from Markdown by Thoxa code.
        </p>
    </section>
}

type Feature(name: String, detail: String)

fn featureList() -> Array<Feature> {
    [
        Feature(name: "Types", detail: "`Int64`, `Int32`, `UInt32`, `UInt64`, `Byte`, `Float64`, `Bool`, `String`, `Bytes`; products and unions; literal types; refinements (`where`); generic types; function types."),
        Feature(name: "Inference", detail: "Local inference for `let`. Return types are inferred for functions and methods without an annotation."),
        Feature(name: "Matching", detail: "Constructor, literal, and or-patterns, with guards. A match on a union must cover every member."),
        Feature(name: "Functions", detail: "Positional or named arguments, defaults, and multi-clause functions that dispatch on parameter types, literal types included."),
        Feature(name: "Values", detail: "Structural `==` on products, unions, and arrays. User types as map keys. `Array` and `Map` copy on write; a `Map` keeps insertion order."),
        Feature(name: "Errors", detail: "`May`, `Trial`, and `?`. A panic ends the task it happens in, not the process."),
        Feature(name: "Memory", detail: "Reference counting. A type that conforms to `Resource` owns something outside the program and releases it with its last reference."),
        Feature(name: "Concurrency", detail: "`-->` starts a task. Socket operations park it until they can proceed. One OS thread."),
        Feature(name: "Data", detail: "`T.read` for JSON text and `T.readForm` for forms; `encode` for output; `Json` literals; typed route body parameters."),
        Feature(name: "Conversions", detail: "`x as T` converts, and its type says whether it can fail; `x as? T` converts or returns the failure. A type adds conversions into itself with `from` clauses."),
        Feature(name: "Web", detail: "Tag literals (`Node`), route clauses, an HTTP/1.1 server with static files and server-sent events, and `sql\"…\"` with SQLite."),
        Feature(name: "C", detail: "`extern fn`, C-layout types, `Address<T>`, and fixed-size arrays."),
        Feature(name: "Tools", detail: "`thoxa run`, `build`, `check` (with `--json`), and `lsp`; a Zed extension."),
    ]
}

fn features() -> Node {
    let chapter = homeChapter()
    <section class="features" id="features">
        <h2>Features</h2>
        <dl>
            {for feature in featureList() {
                <div>
                    <dt>{feature.name}</dt>
                    <dd>{markdown.text(feature.detail, chapter)}</dd>
                </div>
            }}
        </dl>
        <p class="more">The <a href="/manual">manual</a> has the rules for each.</p>
    </section>
}

fn sampleSection(found!: Array<Sample>) -> Node {
    let chapter = homeChapter()
    <section class="samples" id="samples">
        <h2>Samples</h2>
        <p>
            Each sample is a complete program from
            <a href={"{repository}/tree/master/workloads/landing/samples"}>workloads/landing/samples</a>.
            <code>cargo make test</code> runs each one and compares its output with what is
            shown here; the server, which never exits, is compiled but not run.
        </p>
        <nav class="sample-index" aria-label="Samples">
            {for sample in found {
                <a href={"#sample-{sample.name}"}>{sample.title}</a>
            }}
        </nav>
        {for sample in found { sampleView(sample, chapter) }}
    </section>
}

fn sampleView(sample!: Sample, chapter!: Chapter) -> Node {
    <article class="sample" id={"sample-{sample.name}"}>
        <h3>
            {sample.title}
            <a class="anchor" href={"#sample-{sample.name}"} aria-label="Link to this sample">§</a>
        </h3>
        <p>{markdown.text(sample.description, chapter)}</p>
        <pre class="code lang-thx"><code>{highlight.code("thx", sample.code)}</code></pre>
        {output(sample)}
    </article>
}

fn output(sample!: Sample) -> Node {
    if sample.output.isEmpty() {
        <></>
    } else {
        <div class="output">
            <p class="output-label">Output</p>
            <pre><code>{sample.output}</code></pre>
        </div>
    }
}

fn status() -> Node {
    <section class="status" id="status">
        <h2>Status</h2>
        <p>
            Thoxa is pre-alpha. What works is what the test suite covers: each feature above
            has programs in <code>tests/conformance</code> that compile, run, and print what
            they should on every change, under LeakSanitizer. Not there yet:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Releases. Build from source; Linux x86-64 is the only platform.</li>
            <li>Closures, generic functions, and interfaces or traits.</li>
            <li>Threads. Tasks share one OS thread and switch when one parks on a socket.</li>
            <li>Precise memory management. Reference counting is the simple, single-threaded kind: every copy counts.</li>
            <li>A broad standard library. There are files, TCP, HTTP/1.1, JSON, SQLite, strings, paths, and logging.</li>
            <li>Editor support beyond Zed. The language server's navigation is incomplete.</li>
            <li>Stable syntax. Code written today will need changes.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
}

fn building() -> Node {
    let commands = "git clone https://github.com/moonlight-pm/thoxa
cd thoxa
cargo build -p thoxa                  # the compiler: target/rust/debug/thoxa
cargo make test                       # conformance suite, samples, unit tests
target/rust/debug/thoxa run workloads/landing/samples/types.thx"
    <section class="building" id="building">
        <h2>Building</h2>
        <p>You need a Rust toolchain and a C compiler (GCC or Clang).</p>
        <pre class="code lang-bash"><code>{highlight.code("bash", commands)}</code></pre>
        <p>
            The <a href="/manual">manual</a> covers the language, the standard library, and
            the tools. Problems and questions go to the
            <a href={"{repository}/issues"}>issue tracker</a>.
        </p>
    </section>
}

## Links in home page text that name a chapter go to the manual page.
fn homeChapter() -> Chapter {
    Chapter(name: "home", chapters: manual.names, directory: "workloads/landing", page: "/manual")
}
